WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.210
- On this episode and I'm gonna say it,

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.885
the legendary Ken Auletta stops by.

00:00:04.885 --> 00:00:07.468
(groovy music)

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.709
Hey everybody, it's Gary Vaynerchuk

00:00:19.709 --> 00:00:24.480
and this is episode 287
of The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.610
I'm very, very excited for this episode,

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.490
not only is this gentleman my neighbor

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:31.780
in two separate locations,

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.910
but more importantly he's
somebody who I've admired

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:37.990
from afar pretty much as
soon as I started realizing

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.920
there was business content in the world.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.930
And then he embarked on a
book that came out recently

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.200
that went very deep

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.890
into the world of advertising

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.590
and the stuff I was
doing with VaynerMedia,

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.223
and then Beth Comstock, the former CMO of,

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:57.653
and then former Chair you know,

00:00:58.588 --> 00:01:00.940
one of the great big executives
in the corporate world,

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.190
somebody I admire very much,

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.037
texted me one day and said,

00:01:03.037 --> 00:01:04.650
"Hey, I want you to speak to Ken Auletta.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.410
"He's writing a new book
about the industry."

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:09.950
And literally it was a
massive highlight for me

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:13.730
and so sir, thank you so
much for being on this show.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:14.563
- [Ken] Thanks for having me.

00:01:14.563 --> 00:01:18.330
- And so let's do this, because
I think the amazing part

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.130
of this show and the audience

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:24.040
is the dynamics of how many
different kinds of demos

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.010
watch the show.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.170
I'd love to get,

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.040
before we get into the new book,

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:32.317
I'd love to get a two
minute, three minute bio.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:34.770
Tell us about your career.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.280
Because I'd been watching
a bunch of documentaries

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.010
and you keep popping up
which makes me laugh,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.640
and you look the same.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:40.473
- Makes me laugh too.

00:01:40.473 --> 00:01:44.120
- You know black hair, instead
of gray hair, but same face.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.840
- I started, I had a graduate
degree in political science,

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.100
and thought I'd work in
government or something like that.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.230
And I went to work for a guy
by the name of Howard Samuels,

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:57.820
who invented baggies and
the plastic clotheslines,

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:59.090
a business man.
- Interesting.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.280
- An upstate industrialist.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:01.280
He was running for governor.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.980
With my help, he lost.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.589
(laughing)

00:02:04.589 --> 00:02:08.350
So I was at work with Bobby Kennedy's

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.720
1968 presidential campaign.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:11.553
- Well, that's powerful.

00:02:11.553 --> 00:02:12.386
- As a low level,

00:02:12.386 --> 00:02:15.710
- Yeah, that must have been
inspiring and then devastating.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:16.543
- Both, yeah.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.560
And then I went to journalism

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:25.170
and wrote starting with
freelance, then the Village Voice

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.700
and New York Magazine both.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.380
When Murdoch took over,
did a hostile takeover,

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.270
a group of us quit rather than,

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.025
as a protest, rather than work for him.

00:02:34.025 --> 00:02:34.858
- What year was that?

00:02:34.858 --> 00:02:37.520
- That was '77, the winter of '77.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.290
- Was that his first play in the States?

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:40.670
- No, his first play,

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:44.820
he bought the New York Post in '76,

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.970
and promised it would
stay on liberal newspaper,

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.380
and then obviously
reneged on that promise,

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.030
and he had bought the San
Antonio newspaper as well.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.990
But he had done hostile takeovers

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:58.920
in the UK and Australia before,
so he had a bad reputation,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.830
but obviously an interesting businessman.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.490
- [Gary] Sure.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:03.810
- And then I was,

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.980
I went to work both at the New Yorker,

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.320
and writing a column for the Daily News,

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.820
and did TV on WCBS TV,
political commentary,

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.980
and I've written for the
New Yorker since '77,

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.260
and I've since written 12 books.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.093
- 12?

00:03:19.093 --> 00:03:20.460
- This is my 12th book.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.160
- And what was the first book you wrote?

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.180
- It's called The Streets
were Paved with Gold,

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.070
and it was about what
happened to New York City,

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:31.380
not only fiscal crisis, but
the flight of the middle class,

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.913
the working class, the
race, you know polarization.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.760
- And I'm aware of that book,

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.150
how did it do early on from
a successful standpoint?

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:43.800
- It was not a bestseller as
some of the other books were,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.010
but it's a book I'm proud of.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.320
- Did it put you on the map
from a quality standpoint

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:49.730
in the circles you cared about?

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:52.060
- Well actually, you know part of it,

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:54.410
some of it ran in the New York Magazine.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:57.400
This was before the New
Yorker, in like '75,

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.550
and I did a piece, should the
people who hid budget deficits

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.190
in New York City, like
the mayor of New York,

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.180
both Lindsay and Beame,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.960
the Governor of Governor Rockefeller,

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.760
Walter Wriston the head of Citibank,

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.800
some of the union leaders,
did they commit fraud,

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.360
and therefore a crime?

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.560
And Milton Glaser was a name

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.440
you probably know, the great art director

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.870
of New York Magazine, and I said this

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.830
is the investigative piece I've done.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.900
And Milton, right in front of Clay Felker,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.690
the editor of New York Magazine,

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.730
drew what he thought the cover should be,

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:36.860
and it was the mayor of
New York behind bars,

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:40.780
the Governor behind bars,
the bankers behind bars,

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.593
the labor leaders behind bars,

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:47.260
and the headline was, "Should
these people go to jail?"

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.800
And I said, oh my god,

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.360
they've just made this piece come alive.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.210
I was writing a serious
investigative piece

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.010
but he just crystallized
what people would be drawn in

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:58.383
and want to read that.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:00.920
- [Gary] That was a big piece.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:01.780
- That was fun yeah,

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.010
and then I did, you
know just over the years

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:05.943
a number of other pieces.

00:05:05.943 --> 00:05:08.747
- What was the most successful
book you wrote of the 12?

00:05:08.747 --> 00:05:09.691
Like commercially?

00:05:09.691 --> 00:05:11.610
- I think the two
commercially most successful,

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.380
they got up to number
six on the best seller,

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.670
New York Times best seller list,

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:19.770
were the first was Greed
and Glory on Wall Street,

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.270
and that was about the
fall of Lehman Brothers,

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.620
and the greed in the mid-80's
infecting Wall Street.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:31.100
And the next one was in
'91 and it was called,

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:33.940
Three Blind Mice, how the
TV networks lost their way.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.460
How they were being
disrupted by new technology,

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.127
which was cable at the time.

00:05:38.127 --> 00:05:39.970
- And do you see a lot of parallels

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.240
to what's going on with social media

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.280
and things of that nature
to that thesis as well,

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.050
like platform shift of attention?

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:47.883
- Totally.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:53.020
I did a book which also
did well as a seller

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:55.910
called Googled in 2009,

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:57.520
and that was a book about the disruption,

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.280
how Google and the digital
world was disrupting

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.190
the legacy media world,
newspapers, and magazines,

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:04.370
and music, et cetera.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:09.040
And then this current book
is about how the disruption

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.540
that visited legacy media

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:15.130
was now visiting the advertising world.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:17.110
- How did you get to the new book?

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.030
Frenemies, right?

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:19.280
- Yeah, how'd I get to it?

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:20.113
- What's the subtitle?

00:06:20.113 --> 00:06:21.130
It's called Frenemies?

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.586
- The Epic Disruption of the Ad Business

00:06:23.586 --> 00:06:25.080
(and Everything Else).

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:25.913
- [Gary] Yes.

00:06:25.913 --> 00:06:30.210
- And I said, here I am
writing for the New Yorker,

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:31.830
something called the
Annals of Communication

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.544
I follow the media business,

00:06:34.544 --> 00:06:37.490
and yet I don't follow
the advertising business

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.500
which funds the media business.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.850
And if you want to follow
the old Watergate adage,

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:44.750
follow the money, shouldn't I be following

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.390
what's happening with advertising?

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:48.430
And so that set me off to...

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:49.810
- And to frame it up for everybody,

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:50.950
and you know it's really funny,

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:52.210
and a lot of you know who I am.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:54.240
I'm a character that's,

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:55.180
I'm an unusual dude.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.570
I literally genuinely when
I started VaynerMedia,

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:59.700
knew nothing about the advertising world.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.540
I'd never watched Mad Men.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.550
I didn't know how it was structured.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.910
I had no idea that they were mainly run

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:08.010
by publicly traded companies.

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:09.920
I'd never heard of the name Mark.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.210
I mean, this is 2009, I'm 34 years old.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:13.830
I'm a businessman, but
I'm in the wine business.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:15.590
I'm in Silicon Valley investing.

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:17.040
So I had never heard of Martin Sorrell,

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.950
or Omnicom, or Publicis.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:20.590
I didn't know what a
creative director was.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:21.590
I had never heard of,

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.260
as a matter of fact VaynerMedia's,

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:24.093
you would blowin' it,

00:07:24.093 --> 00:07:25.730
the first 40 employees of VaynerMedia,

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:27.840
first of all, it's called VaynerMedia

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.380
and what we did was social media creative.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:31.970
We didn't do any media at the time.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:33.350
I mean I knew that we would.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.950
Number two, the first 40 people hired

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.750
had zero days of advertising experience.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:39.930
- Why'd you hire them?

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:41.780
- I hired them because I knew

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.420
that when I would have conversations

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:45.540
with the one or two people,

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.410
I'd met like three people,
and you know a couple friends

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.010
were like you should hire
somebody from an agency,

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:51.240
I'm like, that makes a whole lot of sense.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:53.780
And I sat down with them in 2009 and '10,

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:55.030
and I'm trying to talk to them

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:58.340
about why we're gonna focus
on Twitter, and Facebook,

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:01.170
and you know, look, I had already built

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.010
a very large wine business.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:06.020
I had already invested in
Facebook, Twitter, Tumblr.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:08.700
I had already had a New York
Times best-selling book.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.270
I was a businessman and I was 35,

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:17.270
and they would try to convince
me on why I needed to focus

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:18.900
on television commercials.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.610
One of the reasons I admire
and like you so much,

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:26.000
is you do two things that I like.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.350
One, you talk about what's coming,

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:31.050
but in actually it's already here,

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:34.700
and that is a very important statement,

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:38.470
and that's where my attachment
is to a lot of what I do.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:41.330
People think that I'm a
disrupter or da-da-da,

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:43.140
this has already happened.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.750
Facebook's happened, it's not coming,

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:49.140
and people for financial
short-term selfish reasons

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.640
hold on to the past, and
they don't acknowledge

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.840
the present, not the future.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.337
And these are, you know,
I speak a lot publicly.

00:08:57.337 --> 00:08:59.610
"Gary Vee's here, he's
a disrupter, dissent."

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.220
First line I'm like, I'm practical.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.640
I'm unemotionally practical.

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:05.800
I want to be historically correct,

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:07.350
and I play for that.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:09.450
So none the less, I
interviewed three people,

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:13.110
they were completely,
completely and utterly incapable

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:14.920
of understanding what
was actually happening,

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:18.020
and they were holding up
the romance of an award,

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:21.370
or an adage article, or
arbitrary Nielsen ratings,

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:24.910
or there was nothing smart
coming out of anybody's mouth

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:28.630
that was senior or
emerging in the ad world,

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:29.590
and I decided, you know what,

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.780
I'm gonna hire a bunch of
kids and I'm gonna teach them.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.940
I'm gonna make sure that
they don't know anything

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:35.060
about that old world.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:37.620
I'm gonna establish a DNA and a culture

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:41.840
of kindness, and curiosity,
and really the currency

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.390
that I believe in, which
is the pulse of the moment.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:46.620
And that's what we did,
and that's what we did.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.780
- You know it's
interesting, every time you,

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:51.330
I've written a lot about
disruption over the years,

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.850
and every time you do that, and you probe,

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:58.740
and you do these interviews,
you find people in legacy media

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.330
who deny the reality of
what is happening to them,

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:04.090
and they do it for lots of reasons.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:06.259
And one of the reasons they do it is,

00:10:06.259 --> 00:10:08.990
Clayton Christensen of
Harvard wrote a great book

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:11.200
several years ago called
The Innovator's Dilemma,

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:14.060
and in it he argues that if
you have an existing business

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:18.170
that is generating money, and
has a reasonable stock price,

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:19.790
the thought that you're gonna divert money

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:24.020
from your existing business
to some digital upstart,

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:26.820
and be punished by crazy Wall Street

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:28.460
because of it, you don't do it,

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:29.760
and you get frozen in place,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.430
and by the time you look
up, your business is gone.

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:34.670
- Ken, let me throw you a
curve, not a curve ball,

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:36.320
let me tell you what I'm fascinated by.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.860
I've always believed that.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:38.880
I see it everyday.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:41.080
I genuinely believe the majority

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:43.630
of the Fortune 500 consumer
package good companies

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:46.493
in America are so in trouble.

00:10:47.446 --> 00:10:49.230
The Kraft's and the
Unilever's and the Heinz,

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.410
you know they are unbelievably in trouble,

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:53.870
because of what's
happening with the Amazon,

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:55.650
what's happening with television, and OTT,

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:58.230
and big box retail, and
they're on the drug of Walmart,

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:01.470
and the channel conflict
wars of the future

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:02.550
are gonna be remarkable.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.910
Which means Pepsi wants to go direct,

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:08.130
but Albertsons and Walmart
and Costco won't let them,

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:11.080
and meanwhile that's gonna be the demise.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:13.300
You're gonna get to that one next.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:14.790
What's fascinating to me is,

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.490
you're right, I've
always been able to pray,

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:20.160
pray on the fact that
people are held accountable

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:23.680
in 90 day windows, and
they will never invest,

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:25.280
