talking about cable television

00:17:11.370 --> 00:17:15.130
with the blind audacity
that she or he did in 19,

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:16.130
when did you write that book?

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:17.448
- It came out in '91.

00:17:17.448 --> 00:17:18.810
It was a five year project.
- In 1991.

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:20.960
First of all, that's
laughable to me to begin with.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:21.793
Here's why.

00:17:21.793 --> 00:17:23.090
Well, five years, that makes sense.

00:17:23.090 --> 00:17:25.380
To me the thought that in 1991,

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:27.210
there was still any debate that cable

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:28.950
was disrupting the big three,

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:31.290
is exactly kind of how I,
you know, look at this.

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:32.700
Look at how many goosebumps I'm getting.

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:33.533
Look at this.

00:17:33.533 --> 00:17:34.980
It's kind of the way I feel right now,

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:38.050
which is how does anybody
debate that Facebook's already,

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:39.880
and social media, and the mobile device

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:42.960
with social platforms eaten up
all the content consumption.

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:45.970
And OTT, by the way the three networks,

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:47.570
now obviously Disney owns ABC,

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:52.470
but as an individual
unit, ABC, CBS, and NBC

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:54.960
are completely out of
business in today's world.

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:57.090
I mean they're not yet,
but they have no place

00:17:57.090 --> 00:17:58.430
outside of live sports.

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:00.060
- But here's what's interesting about it,

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:01.860
and one of the reasons I made Les Moonves,

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:03.670
one of the characters of my book,

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:05.660
I did a chapter where I said,

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:08.480
can old media act like new media?

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:11.770
And I used Moonves and CBS.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:14.480
And arguably, Les Moonves
is the most successful,

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:17.650
modern television executive
in the last quarter century.

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:18.483
- No question.

00:18:18.483 --> 00:18:20.370
- And you look at it, and when I go back

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:23.140
to the book I wrote in
'91, Three Blind Mice,

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:26.600
CBS was 100% reliant, as
were the other networks

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.650
on one source of revenue, advertising.

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:29.483
- [Gary] That's right.

00:18:29.483 --> 00:18:33.410
- Since then, the government
passed the Cable Act in 1992,

00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:35.870
year after my book, which
gave them something called

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:38.730
retransmission consent, which
means the cable companies

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:40.740
had to pay them to run their programs.

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:42.450
CBS last year got a billion dollars

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:44.950
just out of that, new revenue.

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:49.010
In '94, the government passed
FinCEN regulation changes,

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:52.680
which allowed the networks
to own and sell programs,

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:54.270
becomes another source of revenue.

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:56.100
But the third new source of revenue

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:58.820
is with the digital platforms like Netflix

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:00.560
and Amazon coming on.

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:02.140
They decide, the networks,

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:04.609
that they're gonna sell their
programs or their libraries.

00:19:04.609 --> 00:19:06.924
- Yup, the IP that's right
- To them, right?

00:19:06.924 --> 00:19:11.924
And arguably, last year,
it gave $250 million

00:19:11.990 --> 00:19:14.730
each to Fox, and $250 million to CBS.

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:15.667
That's great right?

00:19:15.667 --> 00:19:16.500
- [Gary] Yes.

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:17.510
- But what are they really doing?

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:20.040
And one of the reasons
I used the title Frenemy

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:22.780
in the book is that Netflix is a Frenemy.

00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:24.167
They're a friend in
that they're giving you

00:19:24.167 --> 00:19:26.170
$250 million dollars, but
what are you doing really

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:28.910
when you do that, as old media CBS?

00:19:28.910 --> 00:19:31.050
- You're giving up the last,

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:33.400
the touch-point is the
most important thing.

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:34.233
- Precisely.

00:19:34.233 --> 00:19:35.170
- The touch-point's the game.

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:37.190
Whoever's closest to the consumer wins.

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:40.020
- So if I'm a consumer, I say,

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:42.110
I don't have to wait any
longer for Thursday night

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:44.400
for the program to come on, A.

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:46.150
B, I don't have to watch commercials

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:48.030
on Netflix, or HBO, or Showtime.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:48.863
- [Gary] Of course.

00:19:48.863 --> 00:19:53.863
- And C, I am basically giving
ammunition to my competitors.

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:56.120
- The distribution's been commoditized

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:59.200
because of the internet,
it's now only about the IP.

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:00.033
- [Ken] Right.

